00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:23.800
Data from an Indian lunar orbiter shows signatures consistent with water ice and permanently shadowed

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.260
regions near the moon's poles.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.540
The detection adds to earlier findings from other missions and helps refine target sites

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.240
for future surface operations.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:37.760
Instruments measured reflected light and thermal properties that distinguish ice from dry

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:38.300
regolith.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.320
Next steps include higher resolution mapping to confirm extent and accessibility.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:49.420
This work supports ongoing target selection for robotic exploration and Artemis continuity.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.740
From mapping resources on the moon, we turn to whether similar ice-covered oceans

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.380
elsewhere might support life.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.860
Researchers are developing an ecology-based approach to assess whether subsurface oceans

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.660
on Enceledus and Europa could support life without direct sampling.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.520
The technique models how chemical energy flows through hypothetical ecosystems beneath the

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:12.720
ice.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:19.400
This directly addresses open questions on ocean world biosignatures tracked in the robotic exploration

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:19.680
arc.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.920
The method evaluates habitability by tracking energy availability rather than requiring physical

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.140
samples from the moons.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.720
While we assess where life could exist, agencies are also securing the power sources needed

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.200
to reach those destinations.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:41.600
New facilities are advancing the supply chain for plutonium 238, the radio isotope that power

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.320
spacecraft on long-duration missions beyond the inner solar system.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.860
The effort focuses on reliable fuel production for future probes that cannot rely on solar arrays.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.540
European agencies aim to reduce dependence on external sources for these power systems.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.560
Next milestones include qualification testing of the new production process.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:08.100
With power systems progressing, launch capacity for those missions is also scaling up.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:14.740
The European Space Agency set the next area in six launch for June 17th in its most powerful

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.100
configuration to date.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:23.200
Flight VA 269 will carry 36 satellites for Amazon's project Kuiper constellation.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.600
The rocket's upgraded boosters will deliver greater payload capacity than earlier flights.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.660
Engineers completed final checks on the vehicle and ground systems ahead of the window.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:41.360
A Falcon 9 lifted off at 1540 UTC on June 3rd carrying the latest batch of startling satellites

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:47.500
from Vandenberg Space Force Base. Deployment occurred into a polar orbit to expand coverage over

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:54.140
higher latitudes. While rockets carry new hardware, existing orbiters continue returning fresh data from

00:02:54.140 --> 00:03:00.560
Mars. NASA leaders discuss recent operations and science results from the Maven orbiter during

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.100
a public livestream. The mission has tracked atmospheric loss at Mars for more than a decade.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.640
Data show how solar wind strips gases from the upper atmosphere over time.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.660
The update covered instrument health after years in orbit. Future reports will detail seasonal

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:23.340
